## Migration: `BackgroundProcessor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `while (_running)` loop | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Force stop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `GetNextItem()` blocks | Blocking call interruption | Pass token to unblock |

### Why This Must Change

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. The `volatile bool _running` flag alone can't interrupt a blocked `GetNextItem()` call — that's why the original code resorted to `Abort()`. Cooperative cancellation via `CancellationToken` solves both problems.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)          // replaces volatile bool
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token);           // pass token so blocking unblocks
                ProcessItem(item, token);                // forward through full call chain
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;                                   // exit loop cleanly (replaces ResetAbort)
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel();                                   // replaces Thread.Abort()

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));    // replaces Thread.Sleep(2000) + Abort
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();                                 // CancellationTokenSource is IDisposable
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

---

### What Changed and Why

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `volatile bool _running` | `CancellationToken.IsCancellationRequested` | Token can also interrupt blocking calls |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` | The `when` filter avoids catching unrelated OCEs |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | `break` | You control the loop — just exit it |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Cooperative signal, not forced injection |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` then Abort | `_workerThread.Join(2s)` | Waits for actual completion, no force-kill |
| No disposal | `IDisposable` | `CancellationTokenSource` leaks timers if not disposed |

---

### Critical Pitfalls

1. **`GetNextItem()` must accept the token.** If it blocks on a `Queue`, `Socket`, or `WaitHandle` without checking the token, cancellation stalls. Use `BlockingCollection<T>.Take(token)`, async I/O, or `WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { handle, token.WaitHandle })`.

2. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException` mid-chain.** Only catch it at the top-level loop. If `ProcessItem` catches and swallows it internally, cancellation silently stops working.

3. **`Thread.ResetAbort` → deciding to continue requires a new token.** If you want to skip one failed item and keep processing (like the original `ResetAbort` allowed), replace `break` with `continue` — but create a **new linked `CancellationTokenSource`** per work item:

```csharp
while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
{
    try
    {
        var item = GetNextItem(token);
        // Per-item CTS linked to parent — lets you cancel one item independently
        using var itemCts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(token);
        itemCts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30)); // optional timeout per item
        ProcessItem(item, itemCts.Token);
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        // Per-item timeout — rollback and continue to next item
        RollbackCurrentTransaction();
        continue;
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        // Full shutdown requested
        RollbackCurrentTransaction();
        break;
    }
    finally
    {
        FlushBuffers();
    }
}
```

4. **`Thread.Sleep` in other code paths won't respond to cancellation.** Replace with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` which returns immediately when cancelled.

5. **Always dispose `CancellationTokenSource`.** Implement `IDisposable` on the owning class, as shown above.
